{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-66061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\le -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\le -\\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{1}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\ge \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{8})-1\\ge \\frac{1}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{4}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39d789b0e60ebced329545509f353a3865e7de61838e88a62d7534a6f4d0a9da"} {"question" "q-66061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [3,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,1]\\cup [3,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{3}]\\cup [\\frac{8}{3},\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{2}{3},\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the range of the function $f(x)=5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\le -5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\le -5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $5$ and subtracting $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 1 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})\\ge 5 \\\\  & 5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{4})-3\\ge 5-3 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -8,\\,f(x)\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66062" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39d789b0e60ebced329545509f353a3865e7de61838e88a62d7534a6f4d0a9da"} {"question" "q-66062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-0]\\cup [1,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [8,\\infty )"] ["math" "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{4}{5}]\\cup [2,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66062" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-8]\\cup [2,\\infty )"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\ne n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & \\ne n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{2x}{3} & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{3\\pi }{6}+3n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi  \\\\ x & \\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+3n\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ where $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39d789b0e60ebced329545509f353a3865e7de61838e88a62d7534a6f4d0a9da"} {"question" "q-66071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{5n\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{2}+3n\\pi "] ["math" "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ne \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the domain of the function $f(x)=2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ where $x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The domain of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of all values where $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\ne 0,$ and is given by the expression\n\n$$\nx\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the domain of $2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x-{60}^{∘}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x-{60}^{∘}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x-{60}^{∘}\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-{60}^{∘} & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+{60}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & \\ne {150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & \\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function is $x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66074" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39d789b0e60ebced329545509f353a3865e7de61838e88a62d7534a6f4d0a9da"} {"question" "q-66074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x\\ne {30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}"] ["math" "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}"] ["math" "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}"] ["math" "x\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {30}^{∘}"] ["math" "x\\ne {90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66074" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x\\ne {50}^{∘}+n\\cdot {60}^{∘}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the value of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4$ at its local minimum points?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-1$ are **local maximums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$ For now, we're interested in the local maximums (not local minimums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-2$ and adding $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -1 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 2 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 2+4 \\\\  & -2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+4\\ge 6 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=6$ at its local minimum points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66087" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "39d789b0e60ebced329545509f353a3865e7de61838e88a62d7534a6f4d0a9da"} {"question" "q-66087" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13972064/state.json" "file_sha256" "15f1838164c625ee850f3a24ae1c0c0254a138202d705ec3f20339f6932aa2c7"} {"question" "q-66087" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/De